<Base title="Storage and durability" description="How relational databases store rows in pages, why COMMIT waits for a log, how checkpoints and crash recovery work, and what a crash, a half-written page or a drive that answers too early can take away, with PostgreSQL as the worked example and InnoDB and SQLite compared.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design storage-essay">
    <StorageDefs />
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Storage and durability</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4) and SQLite comparisons" /></header>
<ArticleBody>

    <p><strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that a committed change survives a crash. Once the database tells your application that <code>COMMIT</code> succeeded, the change must still be there after a power cut, a kernel panic or a killed server process. Relational databases keep that promise through the way they store data: how rows sit on disk, how they are copied into memory to be changed, and what is written to disk before the reply.</p>
    <p>Every example follows one change. A shop’s <code>products</code> table holds P7, a blue mug priced at 20.00, and an <code>UPDATE</code> raises it to 22.00.</p>
    <Sql code={update} label="The price change every example follows" />
    <p>Memory is fast and lost in a power cut. Disk survives, but every write to it is slow. So where does P7 live, and what has to reach the disk before the database can say <code>COMMIT</code>?</p>
    <p>In most engines the answer has the same shape. P7 lives inside a fixed-size page, and the engine changes a copy of that page in memory. At <code>COMMIT</code> it appends a small record of the change to a log, and waits until the drive confirms the record is stored. The page itself goes to disk later. If the server crashes first, the engine replays the log to bring the page up to date.</p>
    <p>That wait is the main cost of a commit. On a drive that takes 5 ms to flush, one client can commit at most 200 times a second. Most storage settings trade that wait, and the page writes behind it, against what a crash can take away and how long recovery takes.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="pages"><h2 id="pages">Pages</h2>
      <p>Relational engines store a table as a file of fixed-size <strong>pages</strong>, and every row lives inside one page. To read one row, the engine reads the whole page that holds it. The size differs: <a href={`${docs}storage-page-layout.html`}>PostgreSQL</a> uses 8 kB, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-init-startup-configuration.html`}>InnoDB</a> 16 kB by default (fixed when the instance is created), and <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_page_size`}>SQLite</a> 4 kB by default, adjustable from 512 bytes to 64 kB.</p>
      <p>A row can’t span two pages, so a value too wide for one is stored elsewhere and referenced from the row.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL keeps each table as a <strong>heap</strong>, with rows wherever there is room. InnoDB keeps it as a B-tree ordered by <a href={`${mysql}innodb-index-types.html`}>primary key</a>, and SQLite as one <a href={`${sqlite}fileformat2.html`}>keyed by an integer rowid</a>; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes</a> explains what that changes for a lookup.</p>
      <p>The first drawing shows PostgreSQL’s page layout. Watch an empty table fill, each page from both ends until the two meet. Then try a different row size.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="page-fill"
        number={1}
        title="PostgreSQL rows and line pointers filling three pages"
        alt="Three 8 kB pages drawn as tall columns. Line pointers grow down from each header; rows stack up from the bottom."
        caption="PostgreSQL’s layout, drawn to scale except the 24-byte header, drawn thicker so it stays visible. A row holding 100 bytes of data takes 132 bytes: a 24-byte row header, the data, 4 bytes of padding so the next row starts on an 8-byte boundary, and its 4-byte pointer. The rows have no NULL values."
      />
      <p>With 100 bytes of data, 61 rows fit on a page; with 2,000 bytes, only 4. Every page read brings in all the rows on that page, so narrow rows give more rows for each read.</p>
      <p>The small entries growing down from the header are <strong>line pointers</strong>, one per row. In PostgreSQL a row’s address is its page and its line pointer’s number, which you can read as the hidden <code>ctid</code> column. P7 is at <code>(12,3)</code>: page 12, line pointer 3. A row can move within its page without its address changing, because only its line pointer has to be updated.</p>
      <Sql code={ctid} label="Ask PostgreSQL where P7 is stored" />
      <p>Every index on a table is stored in pages too, and one row change usually touches several. An insert adds an entry to every index. Which indexes an update changes depends on the engine.</p>
      <p>InnoDB, SQL Server and SQLite change only the indexes whose columns changed. In InnoDB every secondary index holds the primary key, so changing the primary key changes them all. PostgreSQL adds an entry to every index unless no indexed column changed and the new row version fits on the same page, as <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/#writes">Indexes</a> explains.</p>
      <p>Each of those pages is changed in memory first and written to disk later, and every change is logged. That is why a table with many indexes writes more log per row, and why a commit that touches one row can still change pages in several places.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="buffers"><h2 id="buffers">Buffer pool</h2>
      <p>No engine reads or changes a page on disk directly. Each keeps a cache of pages in memory, called the <strong>buffer pool</strong>, and works only on the pages in it. To read P7, the engine first needs page 12 in a slot.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL calls its pool <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS`}><strong>shared buffers</strong></a>, shared by every connection, and starts it at 128 MB, or 16,384 pages of 8 kB. InnoDB’s <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool.html`}>buffer pool</a> also defaults to 128 MB. SQLite has no server, so each connection keeps its own page cache, about 2 MB by default.</p>
      <p>Watch a few PostgreSQL queries ask for products. Some need a page from disk and some find it already in memory, and when the slots run out, one page has to make room.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="buffer-read"
        number={2}
        title="PostgreSQL: four buffer slots above the table file"
        alt="Four buffer slots in memory above pages of the table file on disk."
        caption="Shared buffers is PostgreSQL’s name for the buffer pool. Four slots stand in for 16,384. Real lookups also read index pages through the same buffers; this drawing counts only table pages."
      />
      <p>The first query for page 12 paid for a <strong>read</strong>; every later query for it was a <strong>hit</strong> and never left memory. Once every slot was taken, the next read had to <strong>evict</strong> a page to reuse its slot. PostgreSQL’s <a href={`${docs}sql-explain.html`}><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)</code></a> reports these counts on each plan node, in a line such as <code>Buffers: shared hit=3 read=1</code>.</p>
      <p>A read doesn’t always reach the drive, because the operating system keeps its own cache of file data. PostgreSQL leans on that cache, where InnoDB bypasses it. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/#two-caches">The buffer pool</a> explains the two caches and how to size the pool.</p>
      <p>What matters here is that the pool is memory, so a crash empties it. The first queries after a restart read from disk, and a recovered server can be correct and still slow until its working set is back.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="versions"><h2 id="versions">Row versions and undo logs</h2>
      <p>An update can’t simply overwrite P7. A long report that began before the update must still see 20.00, the value as it was when the report started. If the update rolls back, 20.00 has to come back too. So the engine keeps the old version of the row somewhere.</p>
      <p>Where it keeps it is the largest design difference between the engines, and it decides what a long-running transaction costs. The figure runs the same update in PostgreSQL and in InnoDB. A report takes its snapshot first, then transaction 812 changes P7 to 22.00 and commits. Watch where each engine keeps 20.00 for the report, then switch engines.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="row-versions"
        number={3}
        title="Where the old version of P7 waits"
        alt="Page 12 with rows P3, P9 and P7, beside an undo log. Chips marked R and Q show which version a long report and a later query read."
        caption="Both engines give the report 20.00 and the later query 22.00. They differ in where 20.00 waits until no snapshot needs it. The drawing leaves out index entries and InnoDB’s other undo records."
      />
      <p>PostgreSQL wrote a new <a href={`${docs}mvcc-intro.html`}><strong>row version</strong></a> into the table and marked the old one as replaced, so both sat in page 12 for a while. A query decides which one it may see by comparing the transactions that created and replaced each version with the moment its snapshot was taken. <strong>VACUUM</strong> removes the versions no snapshot can see.</p>
      <p>InnoDB <a href={`${mysql}innodb-multi-versioning.html`}>changed the row in place</a> and kept the old value in an <a href={`${mysql}innodb-undo-logs.html`}><strong>undo log</strong></a>. A reader that needs the earlier value rebuilds it from there, and a background <a href={`${mysql}innodb-purge-configuration.html`}>purge</a> discards undo records once no reader needs them.</p>
      <p>SQLite’s default mode keeps old pages only for rollback: its <strong>rollback journal</strong> holds each page’s original contents until the commit, and readers are never given old versions. In <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>WAL mode</a> a reader works from the log as it stood when its read began.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where old versions live and what a long transaction costs"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Old versions live</th><th>What a long transaction costs</th><th>One thing to look at</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>In the table, beside current rows</td><td>Dead versions can’t be removed, so tables and indexes grow. Rolling back is cheap, because the new versions are simply never visible.</td><td>The oldest <code>backend_xmin</code> in <code>pg_stat_activity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>In the undo log</td><td>The undo history grows. Rolling back a huge transaction can take <a href={`${mysql}optimizing-innodb-transaction-management.html`}>several times as long</a> as the change did.</td><td><code>History list length</code> in <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite</td><td>Nowhere in the default mode. In WAL mode, in the log.</td><td>A writer waits for readers before its final write. In WAL mode, a reader that is always open stops a checkpoint from finishing, and the log file grows <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>without bound</a>.</td><td>The size of the <code>-wal</code> file</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</a> follows PostgreSQL’s side on a running server.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dirty"><h2 id="dirty">Dirty pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>dirty page</strong> is a page in the buffer pool that has changed since it was last written to disk. After the update, page 12 in memory holds the 22.00 version, while page 12 in the table file still holds only 20.00. A dirty page has to be written out before its slot can be reused for another page.</p>
      <p>A dirty page may also hold a change from a transaction that has not committed yet. Writing the page doesn’t wait for that commit, so the table files can contain uncommitted changes. After a crash, those changes have to be undone or hidden, using the old versions described above.</p>
      <p>Suppose a database replied to <code>COMMIT</code> while the only copy of the new price was still in memory. The figure shows what that costs.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="dirty-crash"
        number={4}
        title="A database without a log"
        alt="Two lanes: memory, holding page 12 in shared buffers, above disk, holding page 12 in the table file."
        caption="A hypothetical design, shown for contrast. A coral outline and the word ‘dirty’ mark a page whose memory copy differs from the file."
      />
      <p>The application was told the change committed, and the change is gone. The obvious fix is to write page 12 to the table file before replying.</p>
      <p>That is a bad trade. One transaction can dirty pages across the table and its indexes, each a separate write to a different place on disk. And a page half-written when the power fails is part old and part new. So what does the commit write instead?</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="wal"><h2 id="wal">Write-ahead log</h2>
      <p>The commit writes a second record of the change instead, one that is cheap to write: a log. Step through the price change again, this time with a log. Watch what reaches the disk before the reply, and what waits until later.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="wal-commit"
        number={5}
        title="PostgreSQL: the price change with a write-ahead log"
        alt="The same lanes, with WAL buffers added to memory and the WAL file added to disk."
        caption="The drawing keeps the update record in memory until COMMIT; a background flush may write it to disk sooner."
      />
      <p>The log records reached the disk before the reply, and page 12 reached the table file only later. That order gives the log its name. PostgreSQL’s <a href={`${docs}wal-intro.html`}><strong>write-ahead log</strong></a>, or WAL, is a record of every change to a page, precise enough to repeat it. InnoDB’s <a href={`${mysql}innodb-redo-log.html`}>redo log</a> does the same job.</p>
      <p>Both append records to the end of a file and number them with a <strong>log sequence number</strong>, or LSN: a position in the log that only grows. In both, each page remembers the LSN of the last record that changed it. Before a dirty page goes to the table file, the log up to that page’s LSN must already be on disk.</p>
      <p>The table file still said 20.00 when the application heard <code>COMMIT</code>, and that’s fine, because the log on disk can rebuild 22.00.</p>

      <h3 id="writers">Who writes a dirty page</h3>
      <p>If commit writes the log and not the pages, something else must write the pages. Which writer does the work decides where the cost lands.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL has three writers. The checkpointer writes every dirty page on a schedule, so that crash recovery has less log to replay. The background writer writes some dirty pages early, so that queries find clean slots. And a query that needs a slot, and finds the page chosen for eviction dirty, must write that page out itself before it can read.</p>
      <p>The figure fills Figure 2’s buffer pool with dirty pages and sends it one more query. Choose whether the background writer kept up, and watch who waits for the write.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="dirty-evict"
        number={6}
        title="PostgreSQL: who writes the dirty page a query needs to evict"
        alt="The four buffer slots from Figure 2, all marked dirty, above the table file. A query needs page 13, which isn’t in memory."
        caption="PostgreSQL’s writers. The checkpointer also cleans pages as it works, which spares queries in the same way. Whoever writes a page, the log up to its LSN must reach the disk first."
      />
      <p>When the background writer fell behind, the query’s read had to wait for a write first. When it kept up, the same query paid for the read alone. Only a query’s own write makes the query wait, so who does the writing matters more than how many pages are dirty.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>page cleaner threads</a> do this work continuously, and flush faster as the pool gets dirtier and the redo log fills. SQLite has no background writer: the connection that commits writes its own pages. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/#writers">Watching storage</a> shows how to count each kind of writer.</p>

      <h3 id="commit-wait">What COMMIT waits for</h3>
      <p>While a transaction runs, its log records collect in a buffer in memory: the WAL buffers in PostgreSQL, the log buffer in InnoDB. At <code>COMMIT</code> the engine appends a commit record, writes the buffer to the log file, and asks the operating system to flush the file. Only when the flush returns does it reply.</p>
      <p>The flush is the slow step, because it waits for the storage to confirm. So a single client committing one row at a time can’t commit faster than the storage flushes, however fast the processor is.</p>
      <p>On one laptop’s SSD, with PostgreSQL set to make the drive really flush, inserting one row per transaction gave these rates. They show the pattern, not typical numbers.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed commit rates on one laptop"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Commits per second</th><th>Time per commit</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 client, full flush</td><td>about 190</td><td>5.1 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>16 clients, full flush</td><td>about 1,570</td><td>10.2 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 client, <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> (limited by the processor, not the storage)</td><td>about 72,000</td><td>0.014 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>16 clients, <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> (limited by the processor, not the storage)</td><td>about 170,000</td><td>0.09 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000 rows in one transaction</td><td>one commit</td><td>8 to 12 ms in all</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>One client is held to the flush time: 5.1 ms a commit is 196 commits a second. Sixteen clients get eight times the throughput from the same drive, and each commit takes twice as long. Turning the wait off, or committing ten thousand rows at once, removes the flush from the path almost entirely. Batching is the application’s choice. Turning the wait off is a setting with a price: <a href="#settings">asynchronous commit</a>.</p>

      <h3 id="group-commit">Group commit</h3>
      <p>Sixteen clients get eight times the throughput because their commits share flushes. Their commit records sit in the same log buffer, so one flush makes all of them durable, and every one of them gets its reply.</p>
      <p>A commit that arrives during a flush waits for the rest of it and then the whole of the next. With every client committing again the moment it’s answered, nearly every commit arrives just as a flush starts, so each waits almost two: 10.2 ms, twice 5.1.</p>
      <p>Sharing a flush this way is called <strong>group commit</strong>. PostgreSQL does it, and InnoDB does it for its <a href={`${mysql}glossary.html`}>redo log and its binary log</a>. The figure models the ceiling it sets. Choose how many clients are committing, how long a flush takes and whether commits share flushes. With several clients, watch them settle into two groups that take turns.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="group-commit"
        number={7}
        title="Commits sharing log flushes"
        alt="Four log flushes in a row, each labelled with the number of commits it carries."
        caption="In this model each client commits again as soon as it is answered, and processor time is ignored. With 16 clients and a 5 ms flush, each flush carries one group of 8: 1,600 commits a second, close to the 1,570 observed above. Real engines always share flushes; turning sharing off shows what it saves."
      />
      <p>So flush latency sets commit latency. How many commits a second a log can sustain depends on the flush time and on how many clients commit together. A drive with a slow flush limits a write-heavy workload more than its raw bandwidth would suggest. SQLite has nothing to group, because it allows one writer at a time.</p>

      <h3 id="records">Log records and MySQL’s binary log</h3>
      <p>Redo and write-ahead records describe changes to pages, in a form precise enough to repeat. They are not SQL statements. Replaying a statement against whatever data happens to be there could give a different answer; replaying a page change gives the same page.</p>
      <p>MySQL also keeps a <a href={`${mysql}binary-log.html`}>binary log</a>, a separate record of changes written for replicas and for point-in-time recovery. It describes changes in terms of the data rather than the pages. Both logs are written at commit.</p>

      <h3 id="sqlite-modes">SQLite’s rollback journal and WAL mode</h3>
      <p>SQLite’s default mode works the other way round. Before changing a page, it saves the page’s <em>original</em> contents in a rollback journal. It then writes the new pages into the database file, flushes them, and deletes the journal. The deletion is the commit.</p>
      <p><a href={`${sqlite}atomiccommit.html`}>After a crash</a>, a journal that is still there means the commit never finished, and SQLite copies the saved pages back. A commit in this mode needs several flushes: two for the journal and one for the database file.</p>
      <p>SQLite’s WAL mode works like the logs above instead. Commits append page frames to a separate file, and a frame marked as the end of a transaction is the commit. The first connection to open the database after a crash reads the log, ignoring frames whose checksums fail. WAL mode writes the content once instead of twice, which is a large part of why it is faster.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="recovery"><h2 id="recovery">Crash recovery</h2>
      <p><strong>Crash recovery</strong> replays the log after a crash to bring the table files up to date. It doesn’t start at the beginning of the log. It starts at the last <strong>checkpoint</strong>: a log position before which every change is already in the table files.</p>
      <p>Each record is applied only where it is missing. Recovery compares the record’s LSN with the LSN on the page it changes. If the page is older, the change is missing and recovery applies it. If the page already has it, recovery skips it, so no change is applied twice.</p>
      <p>What the crash costs depends on what it destroyed. A killed server process leaves the operating system’s cache intact, so everything the server had written survives, flushed or not. A kernel panic or a power cut keeps only what had been flushed. The figure cuts the power at one of three moments. Try all three, and watch what the application sees in each.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="crash-recovery"
        number={8}
        title="PostgreSQL: crash recovery at three moments"
        alt="The lanes from figure 5, with the power failing at the chosen moment."
        caption="Page 12 had already changed once since the last checkpoint, so the earlier records include it. Records still in memory at the crash are gone."
      />
      <p>When the commit record had reached the disk, recovery found page 12 older than the update record and applied it, so P7 came back at 22.00. When the power failed before the flush, nothing of the update survived. In both cases the application saw only a dropped connection, the same whether its change was kept or lost.</p>

      <h3 id="unfinished">Unfinished transactions</h3>
      <p>Recovery brings back exactly the transactions whose commit records reached disk. The log may also hold records of transactions that never committed, and what happens to those depends on where old versions live. PostgreSQL replays them like any others, and their versions stay invisible: with no commit record in the log, the transaction counts as aborted, so no snapshot is allowed to see its versions.</p>
      <p>InnoDB <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>replays the redo log first</a>, which restores the unfinished changes too, and then rolls those transactions back using the undo log. It accepts connections before that rollback finishes, so new connections can briefly meet locks held by transactions that are being undone.</p>
      <p>A transaction that had changed millions of rows takes correspondingly long to undo. Killing the process doesn’t help: the rollback starts again on startup. SQLite’s recovery is the journal restore or the checksummed log described above.</p>

      <h3 id="recovery-time">Recovery time</h3>
      <p>Replay time is roughly the amount of log from the last checkpoint to the end of the log, divided by how fast the server can apply it. That is why checkpoint settings set recovery time.</p>
      <p>On the same laptop, an update-heavy test wrote 566 MiB of log in 20 seconds, about 28 MiB a second, and the server was then stopped as if it had crashed. Replaying that log took 3.35 seconds, about 169 MiB a second. Another 0.56 seconds went on a checkpoint at the end of recovery, before the server accepted connections.</p>
      <p>Treat that rate as a ceiling. The log in that test was mostly <strong>full-page images</strong>, copies of whole pages that PostgreSQL logs the first time each page changes after a checkpoint, and they replay quickly. The files were also in the operating system’s cache. After a reboot, or on a standby that has just started, replay can be much slower. Your own rate is worth measuring the same way, by crashing a copy under load.</p>
      <p>Replay is only part of the outage. The server also has to notice the crash and restart, InnoDB may still be rolling back a large transaction, and the cache starts cold. A recovery-time target should include all four.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s server log reports where replay began (<code>redo starts at</code>), where it ended (<code>redo done at</code>) and how long replay took. The difference between the two positions is the log replayed. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/#restart">Reading a restart</a> goes through that log line by line, and through InnoDB’s phases.</p>

      <h3 id="cannot-repair">What recovery cannot repair</h3>
      <p>Recovery replays the log it finds, so it fixes only what the log can fix. Four kinds of loss are beyond it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Commits whose log records were never really on disk, which is what a drive that acknowledged a flush early causes.</li>
        <li>Damage that is not a tear, such as a bit flip or a bad sector. PostgreSQL and InnoDB detect it with checksums, but replaying the log does not rewrite the page; the remedy is a copy from elsewhere.</li>
        <li>Missing or replaced files: log files deleted by hand, a SQLite journal or <code>-wal</code> file <a href={`${sqlite}howtocorrupt.html`}>moved away from its database</a>, or a data directory restored without its log.</li>
        <li>A lost disk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Checking the drive prevents the first, as the section on <code>fsync</code> explains. Backups cover the rest.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checkpoints"><h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
      <p>Each checkpoint writes out dirty pages and moves the checkpoint position forward. Recovery then has less log to replay, and the log before that position can be reused. Without checkpoints, recovery would replay the whole history and the log would grow without limit.</p>
      <p>Every engine therefore trades three costs against each other. Recovery time grows with the log since the last checkpoint. Page writes grow with checkpoint frequency, because a page that changes again after a checkpoint has to be written again. And in PostgreSQL, log volume grows with checkpoint frequency too. What differs between engines is how they move the position forward.</p>

      <h3 id="rounds-or-continuous">Periodic and fuzzy checkpoints</h3>
      <p>PostgreSQL checkpoints periodically, in rounds. Every 5 minutes (<code>checkpoint_timeout</code>), or sooner, once waiting longer could take the log past <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB), it picks a position, writes every page dirtied before it, and <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>records the position</a>. It spreads the writes over most of the interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>, 0.9) so the disk is not hit all at once.</p>
      <p>InnoDB moves the position continuously. Its <a href={`${mysql}innodb-checkpoints.html`}>fuzzy checkpointing</a> flushes dirty pages in small batches in the background, and the checkpoint advances behind the oldest page still dirty, so there are no rounds.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s redo log has a fixed capacity, so the position can’t fall too far behind. When writes fill the log faster than <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>the flushing keeps up</a>, a <strong>sharp checkpoint</strong> forces dirty pages out, and writes stall until space is free.</p>
      <p>SQLite in WAL mode is the simplest: it checkpoints when the log reaches 1,000 pages, and that checkpoint, like every automatic one, does not wait for readers.</p>
      <p>The figure runs both models over the same forty price changes, for products on pages 10 to 15. Choose the model and how often it acts. Then compare the replay, the page writes and, for PostgreSQL, the full-page images.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="checkpoints"
        number={9}
        title="Checkpoints in rounds and continuously"
        alt="Six pages in shared buffers, marked clean or dirty, above a grid of log records labelled with their pages. C marks a checkpoint."
        caption="Checkpoints here are instant and counted in records; real ones are timed and spread out. The continuous model flushes the pages that have been dirty longest after every batch, which is a simplification of InnoDB’s pacing. InnoDB doesn’t log full-page images, so the continuous model counts none."
      />
      <p>In the rounds model, a checkpoint every 5 records leaves 5 records to replay after the crash, at the price of 30 page writes and 35 full-page images. A checkpoint every 20 records leaves 20 to replay, with 6 writes and 12 images.</p>
      <p>The more often PostgreSQL checkpoints, the more full-page images it logs. Rare checkpoints cost less in writes and log volume, and more in recovery time.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s model writes fewer pages for the same changes, 24 against 30 at every 5 records, because a page that is still changing stays in memory until it has been dirty a while.</p>
      <p>But some log always remains to replay, because the checkpoint position trails the oldest page still dirty: 7 records against 5. At every 20 records the two models give the same numbers, because by then every dirty page is old enough to write.</p>

      <h3 id="checkpoint-settings">Checkpoint settings</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Checkpoint settings by engine"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>What starts a checkpoint</th><th>Settings that matter</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Time, or log size</td><td><code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (5 min), <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB), <code>checkpoint_completion_target</code> (0.9), <code>min_wal_size</code> (80 MB), <code>checkpoint_warning</code> (30 s)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>Nothing in particular: flushing is continuous and speeds up as the log fills or the pool gets dirty</td><td><code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code> (100 MiB), <code>innodb_max_dirty_pages_pct</code> (90), <code>innodb_max_dirty_pages_pct_lwm</code> (10), <code>innodb_adaptive_flushing</code> (on), <code>innodb_adaptive_flushing_lwm</code> (10), <code>innodb_io_capacity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, WAL mode</td><td>Log size</td><td><code>PRAGMA wal_autocheckpoint</code> (1,000 pages); manual modes PASSIVE, FULL, RESTART and TRUNCATE</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3 id="choosing-interval">Sizing the log between checkpoints</h3>
      <p>Start from a recovery-time target and work backwards. Say a restart may spend 30 seconds replaying. At the 169 MiB a second measured above, that is about 5 GiB of log.</p>
      <p>Next, find how much log a crash can leave to replay. PostgreSQL wants each checkpoint to finish before the log reaches <code>max_wal_size</code>, and the log keeps growing while a checkpoint spreads its writes. So it starts the next checkpoint once the log written since the previous checkpoint began reaches the setting divided by one plus the share of the interval spent writing:</p>
      <p class="formula">trigger = max_wal_size ÷ (1 + checkpoint_completion_target)</p>
      <p>At the defaults, the trigger is about half the setting: 1 GB (1,024 MiB) divided by 1.9 is about 540 MiB. The server counts whole 16 MiB log segments and rounds down, so in practice a checkpoint starts after 512 to 528 MiB. The rule isn’t documented, so it may change between versions.</p>
      <p>The trigger is not the worst case. A crash just before a checkpoint finishes has to replay from where the previous checkpoint began. That is one trigger’s worth of log, plus everything written while the current checkpoint ran: close to <code>max_wal_size</code> in all.</p>
      <p>The figure draws this at about 28 MiB of log a second, the rate of the update-heavy test above. Watch the log a crash would replay climb until a checkpoint finishes, then drop. Then move the slider to change <code>max_wal_size</code>, and choose a replay rate: the measured 169 MiB a second, or half that.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="checkpoint-sizing"
        number={10}
        title="PostgreSQL: how max_wal_size sets checkpoint spacing and the worst-case replay"
        alt="A chart over five minutes of how much log a crash would leave to replay, rising between checkpoints and dropping when each one finishes, against a dotted line at max_wal_size and a dashed line for what replays in 30 seconds."
        caption="Computed from PostgreSQL’s trigger rule for a steady 28 MiB of log a second, with every checkpoint finishing on schedule and no rounding to whole segments. In max_wal_size, 1 GB means 1,024 MiB. At this write rate, log size always starts a checkpoint before the 5-minute timer would."
      />
      <p>At the default 1 GB, a checkpoint starts every 19 seconds or so, and the worst case replays in about 6 seconds, far inside the target. The real server agreed. When the same load ran for 45 seconds, checkpoints started at a <code>distance</code> of about 516 MiB, every 20 seconds or so, and the server log objected:</p>
      <pre class="plan" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="PostgreSQL warning about frequent checkpoints"><code>{checkpointWarning}</code></pre>
      <p>That leaves plenty of room under the 30-second target. The cost is elsewhere: a checkpoint every 20 seconds writes the same dirty pages over and over, and after each one the next change to every page logs a full-page image again.</p>
      <p>Raising <code>max_wal_size</code> trades that cost for longer replay. At 4 GB, a checkpoint starts after about 2.1 GiB of log, about every 77 seconds, and the worst case replays in about 24 seconds at 169 MiB a second, inside 30. At half that rate it would take about 48.</p>
      <p>The method carries over to any server, as long as checkpoints keep up. Replay rate times the time you can spend gives a ceiling for <code>max_wal_size</code>, and the observed <code>distance</code> shows how close the settings run to it. But <code>max_wal_size</code> is a soft limit: a checkpoint the disks can’t finish in time lets the log, and the replay, grow past it, so leave room below the ceiling.</p>
      <p>In InnoDB the redo log capacity plays the same part, because the checkpoint trails the end of the log by at most that much. MySQL 8.4 can resize it while running. Flushing speeds up as the log fills: <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>adaptive flushing</a> starts at 10% of the capacity (<code>innodb_adaptive_flushing_lwm</code>), faster flushing at 75%, and a full log forces the sharp checkpoint described above.</p>
      <p>To size it, sample the log position twice and divide the difference by the seconds between the samples: that is the redo InnoDB writes per second. The capacity divided by it is how long the log lasts before flushing must catch up. For example, a server writing an invented 60 MiB of redo a second would use up the default 100 MiB in under 2 seconds.</p>
      <p>Choose a capacity that covers the longest burst of writing you want to absorb without pages being forced out, and keep the gap between the two positions well below 75% of it.</p>
      <Sql code={innodbLsn} label="Read InnoDB's redo log and checkpoint positions" />

      <h3 id="seeing-checkpoints">Monitoring checkpoints</h3>
      <p>The decision above turns on one count: how many checkpoints the timer started and how many the log size did. <code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code> keeps both. Many more <code>num_requested</code> than <code>num_timed</code> means log size is forcing checkpoints early. PostgreSQL also says so in the server log, with the warning shown earlier, whenever two such checkpoints start closer together than <code>checkpoint_warning</code>.</p>
      <Sql code={checkpointer} label="Count timed and requested checkpoints in PostgreSQL" />
      <p>With <code>log_checkpoints</code> on, which it is by default, each checkpoint also writes a pair of lines to the server log. They say what started it, how much it wrote, how long the writing and the flush took and how much log it covered.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s counterpart is the gap between <code>Innodb_redo_log_current_lsn</code> and <code>Innodb_redo_log_checkpoint_lsn</code>, read against the capacity. SQLite’s is the result of <code>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</code>, which says whether the checkpoint was blocked. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/#checkpoint-line">Watching storage</a> reads all three, field by field.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Checkpoint symptoms and what to change"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to change</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Server log says checkpoints are occurring too frequently</td><td>PostgreSQL is starting checkpoints because of log size, closer together than <code>checkpoint_warning</code></td><td>Raise <code>max_wal_size</code>, then check recovery time against your target</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Latency rises at regular intervals</td><td>Checkpoint writes competing with queries</td><td>The writes are already spread over 90% of the interval, so give the I/O more capacity, or lengthen the interval if recovery time allows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB writes stall now and then when busy</td><td>The redo log is nearly full and a sharp checkpoint is forcing pages out</td><td>Raise <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>; check <code>innodb_io_capacity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>The SQLite <code>-wal</code> file keeps growing</td><td>A reader is open across checkpoints, so none can finish</td><td>Find the long reader, or run a RESTART or TRUNCATE checkpoint in a gap between readers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="log-volume"><h2 id="log-volume">Log volume and retention</h2>
      <p>The log is a cost as well as a protection. It is written on every commit, shipped to replicas and archives, and kept until nothing needs it. Four things set how much of it there is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>How many pages a change touches, because every changed page is logged and every index is more pages.</li>
        <li>How many changes there are, and how wide their rows.</li>
        <li>In PostgreSQL, the full-page images logged after each checkpoint, which is why frequent checkpoints inflate the log.</li>
        <li>In PostgreSQL, rewrites that give back space, such as <code>VACUUM FULL</code>, which write and log the whole table again.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Log that is no longer needed for recovery can be reused, but several things can hold it back.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What keeps log files and what happens when they pile up"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Log</th><th>Kept until</th><th>If it piles up</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL WAL</td><td>A checkpoint has passed it and, with archiving on, the archive command has copied it</td><td>Files <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>accumulate</a> while archiving falls behind</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL WAL held by a replication slot</td><td>The slot’s consumer, a standby or a logical replication client, has confirmed it</td><td>No limit by default: <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-replication.html`}><code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code></a> is -1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB redo log</td><td>The checkpoint has passed it</td><td>It can’t grow past its capacity, so a sharp checkpoint stalls writes instead</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MySQL binary log</td><td>30 days have passed (<code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>), whether or not anything needs it</td><td>A busy server can fill its disk with binary logs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite <code>-wal</code> file</td><td>A checkpoint has copied its frames into the database</td><td>The file grows</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The common incident is the PostgreSQL one. The log directory fills the disk because a slot or an archive command stopped consuming, and the server can no longer write, which stops every commit. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/#retention">What holds the log back</a> shows how to see which of these it is.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="torn-pages"><h2 id="torn-pages">Torn pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>torn page</strong> is a page that was only partly written when the power failed. A database writes a whole page at a time, but a drive only promises to write each sector whole, traditionally 512 bytes. That makes a PostgreSQL page 16 sectors and an InnoDB page 32, and the power can fail between any two of them.</p>
      <p>Drives with larger sectors tear less often, but a page is still more than one sector. A log record that says “change these bytes” can’t be applied to a page that is half old and half new, so every engine needs a way to get a whole page back.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s way is <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-FULL-PAGE-WRITES`}><code>full_page_writes</code></a>, on by default. The first time a page changes after a checkpoint, the log record for that change carries a full-page image, a copy of the whole page. Watch a page write fail partway, with the setting on and then off.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="torn-page"
        number={11}
        title="PostgreSQL: a torn page and its full-page image"
        alt="Page 12 as sixteen 512-byte sectors, in shared buffers and in the table file, with its WAL record below."
        caption="New sectors are striped; old ones are plain. Drives don’t promise to write sectors in order; the drawing shows them in order for clarity."
      />
      <p>With the image in the log, recovery doesn’t need the torn page at all. It copies the image over it and replays later changes on top. One image per checkpoint is enough: recovery always starts at the last checkpoint, so the first record it meets for a page is the one carrying the image.</p>
      <p>With the setting off, the record holds only the change, and recovery has no clean copy to fall back on. If data checksums are on, the torn page fails its check and recovery stops with an invalid-page error. Without them, the new LSN in the page’s first sector makes it look up to date, so recovery skips the record and the tear goes unnoticed.</p>
      <p>InnoDB keeps the copy somewhere else. Before it writes a page to its place in the data file, it writes the page to a <a href={`${mysql}innodb-doublewrite-buffer.html`}>doublewrite buffer</a>, on by default. If the write is torn, recovery finds a good copy there and then applies the redo log.</p>
      <p>SQLite never overwrites a page it can’t restore. The rollback journal holds the original, and in WAL mode the main file changes only at a checkpoint. A checkpoint doesn’t discard the log until the file has been flushed, so a checkpoint torn by a crash is redone from the log.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where each engine keeps the clean copy of a page"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Where the clean copy lives</th><th>When it is written</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>A full-page image in the log</td><td>At the first change to a page after a checkpoint</td><td>Log volume, up to 8 kB per page per checkpoint</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>The doublewrite buffer</td><td>Every time a page is flushed</td><td>A second write of every page flushed, to a sequential area</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, rollback journal</td><td>The journal holds the original page</td><td>Before the page is changed</td><td>An extra file write and flushes at every commit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, WAL mode</td><td>The log still holds the frames</td><td>At commit; the log is kept until the checkpoint is flushed</td><td>Checkpoint writes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The protection can sometimes be turned off. ZFS prevents partial page writes, so <code>full_page_writes</code> can be off on it. InnoDB’s <code>innodb_doublewrite</code> can be off, or set to detect only, and the buffer turns itself off on Fusion-io devices that support atomic writes.</p>
      <p>Turning it off is a decision to rely on the storage writing a page atomically. If you cannot show that it does, the safe choice is to leave it on and accept the cost.</p>

      <h3 id="checksums">Page checksums</h3>
      <p>Detecting damage is a separate job from repairing it. A checksum stored with each page tells the engine that a page is wrong; only a clean copy fixes it. A checksum also catches damage that is not a tear, such as a bit flip on the drive, which no full-page image or doublewrite can repair.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s data checksums are on by default for clusters created by <code>initdb</code> in version 18, and it counts failures in <code>pg_stat_database.checksum_failures</code>. InnoDB’s <code>innodb_checksum_algorithm</code> defaults to <code>crc32</code>. SQLite does not checksum database pages by default; its WAL frames carry checksums, and an optional <a href={`${sqlite}cksumvfs.html`}>checksum extension</a> adds a per-page one.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="flush"><h2 id="flush"><code>fsync</code> and drive caches</h2>
      <p>Every guarantee so far rests on one report: the operating system saying that a flush has finished. PostgreSQL, InnoDB and SQLite all rely on it, and if it is wrong, a committed transaction can vanish.</p>

      <h3 id="flush-asks">What <code>fsync</code> asks for</h3>
      <p>To make a log record durable, a database writes it to the log file and then calls <code>fsync</code>. That asks the operating system to put the file’s data on durable storage and to return only when it has. On Linux, PostgreSQL uses the variant <code>fdatasync</code> by default, which skips file metadata the log doesn’t need.</p>
      <p>Two caches sit between the database and the storage itself. The operating system’s page cache holds data the database has handed over but not yet flushed. The drive has its own write cache, and on many drives that cache is ordinary volatile memory. Follow the commit record down, first with a drive that honours flush requests, then with one that doesn’t.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="flush-path"
        number={12}
        title="PostgreSQL: the flush path"
        alt="Four layers, top to bottom: WAL buffers, the operating system page cache, the drive’s write cache and its flash cells."
        caption="The top three layers are memory. An operating-system crash loses the top two; a database process crash loses only the first."
      />
      <p>The database couldn’t notice anything wrong with the second drive. The drive simply reported the data safe while it sat in volatile memory.</p>

      <h3 id="flush-breaks">Write caches and power-loss protection</h3>
      <p><a href={`${docs}wal-reliability.html`}>PostgreSQL</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-parameters.html`}>MySQL</a> and <a href={`${sqlite}atomiccommit.html`}>SQLite</a> all warn that many drives acknowledge a flush before the data is safe. The layers where data can be lost, and what protects each, are these.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Layers where flushed data can be lost and what protects them"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What loses data there</th><th>What protects it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Database buffer</td><td>A crash of the server process, or anything worse</td><td>The commit waits for the flush</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Operating-system cache</td><td>A kernel panic or power loss</td><td><code>fsync</code> before replying</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Controller cache</td><td>Power loss, if the cache has no battery</td><td>A battery or flash-backed cache, or a write-through setting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Drive cache</td><td>Power loss, if the drive acknowledged the flush early</td><td>Power-loss protection, or the cache turned off</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Virtual or network volume</td><td>Whatever the hypervisor or storage service does before it acknowledges a write</td><td>The provider’s documented meaning of an acknowledged write</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Drives sold with <strong>power-loss protection</strong> hold enough stored energy to finish writing their cache, so their cache is safe to count on.</p>
      <p>On a virtual machine or a network volume, the layers are the provider’s. The guarantee is whatever its documentation says an acknowledged write means, so read it for the volume type you use. Local instance storage, for example, may disappear when an instance stops, whatever it acknowledged.</p>
      <p>A flush can also fail. If the operating system could not write a page back, a retry may report success even though the data was dropped, which would hide the lost write. PostgreSQL treats this as fatal: with <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-error-handling.html`}><code>data_sync_retry</code></a> off, the default, a failure to flush the data files raises a PANIC, and the server restarts and recovers from the log.</p>

      <h3 id="flush-check">Checking your own storage</h3>
      <p>Counting flushes gives a rough test for a spinning disk. At 7,200 rpm it turns 120 times a second, so it can complete at most about 120 flushes a second, and a result in the thousands means a cache is answering. The test fails for SSDs: an honest drive with power-loss protection can flush thousands of times a second, so a high count alone proves nothing.</p>
      <p>Two tools measure what a flush costs. PostgreSQL’s <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}><code>pg_test_fsync</code></a> times each flush method on your volume. <code>fio</code> with <code>--fsync=1</code> measures a write followed by a flush on any volume, at the block size and concurrency you choose. Neither tells you whether the flush is honest.</p>
      <p>What does is pulling the plug during a write test, or the drive’s documented power-loss protection. <code>diskchecker.pl</code> is a script built for that test. On a managed service such as Amazon RDS or Google Cloud SQL, the provider chooses the drives, so these hardware checks are theirs to make. The commit settings, which decide whether <code>COMMIT</code> waits for the flush, are still yours.</p>
      <p>The flush time is also the floor for commit latency, as the table of commit rates showed. On the laptop behind those rates, a commit took 5 ms when the drive flushed for real. With PostgreSQL’s default flush method it took 0.04 ms, because macOS acknowledges a plain flush before the drive has finished. SQLite offers <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_fullfsync`}><code>PRAGMA fullfsync</code></a> for the same reason.</p>
      <p>Local flash with power-loss protection, a network-attached volume and a spinning disk can differ widely here, and so do the commit rates they allow. No single typical flush time holds across them, so measure the volume you run on, under the load you expect.</p>

      <h3 id="flush-watch">Flush time in production</h3>
      <p>PostgreSQL can time its log flushes. With <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> on, the row of <code>pg_stat_io</code> for the <code>wal</code> object reports how many flushes each backend type made and how long they took. Sessions waiting on a flush show the wait event <code>WalSync</code>.</p>
      <p>InnoDB counts the flushes of its redo log in <code>Innodb_os_log_fsyncs</code>. In either engine, the application’s own commit latency, at the percentile you care about, is the number the flush finally limits. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/">Watching storage in a running database</a> reads these in an incident.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="settings"><h2 id="settings">Asynchronous commit</h2>
      <p>Waiting for a flush at every commit is the expensive part of a write, as the commit rates above showed. So every engine lets the reply come before the flush. This is <strong>asynchronous commit</strong>, and the trade is the same everywhere: a crash can lose the most recent commits, and nothing else. The settings differ in name and in grain.</p>

      <h3 id="three-settings">The setting in each engine</h3>
      <p>In PostgreSQL, <a href={`${docs}wal-async-commit.html`}><code>synchronous_commit = off</code></a> replies as soon as the commit record is in memory, and a background process, the WAL writer, flushes it soon after. The WAL writer wakes every 200 ms by default (<code>wal_writer_delay</code>). Because it prefers to write whole pages when busy, the longest risk window is three times that delay: about 600 ms at the default. You can set it for a single transaction.</p>
      <p>Here is the price change with PostgreSQL’s setting off. Choose whether the power fails before or after the background flush.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="async-commit"
        number={13}
        title="PostgreSQL: the price change with synchronous_commit off"
        alt="The lanes from figures 5 and 8, with synchronous_commit off."
        caption="The same model as figures 5 and 8, with one setting changed."
      />
      <p>The reply came before the flush. When the power failed first, the application had been told about a commit that recovery could not bring back, yet the database itself stayed consistent.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s setting is <a href={`${mysql}innodb-parameters.html`}><code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit</code></a>. Its three values decide what each commit writes, and so what a crash can take.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each value of innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit risks"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>At each commit</th><th>If <code>mysqld</code> crashes</th><th>If the power fails or the OS crashes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1, the default</td><td>Writes the redo log and flushes it</td><td>Nothing lost</td><td>Nothing lost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Writes the redo log to the operating system; InnoDB flushes it about once a second, sometimes later (<code>innodb_flush_log_at_timeout</code>)</td><td>Nothing lost: the operating system still holds the writes</td><td>About a second of commits, sometimes more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>Nothing; InnoDB writes and flushes the redo log on the same timer</td><td>About a second of commits, sometimes more</td><td>About a second of commits, sometimes more</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>With the binary log on, which is the default in 8.4, a commit also writes the binary log. With <a href={`${mysql}replication-options-binary-log.html`}><code>sync_binlog</code></a> and <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit</code> both at 1, their defaults, a commit flushes both logs. After a crash, InnoDB completes or rolls back each prepared transaction so that the two logs agree.</p>
      <p>With <code>sync_binlog = 0</code>, the server leaves flushing the binary log to the operating system, and a power cut or an operating-system crash can lose its last events. That can go wrong in two directions.</p>
      <p>The database can keep committed transactions that its binary log lacks. Replicas and point-in-time recovery read the binary log, so those transactions never reach them.</p>
      <p>Or a replica may already have read events that the source then lost. The replica then holds commits the source no longer has.</p>
      <p>In SQLite, the defaults are the rollback journal (<code>journal_mode = DELETE</code>) and <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_synchronous`}><code>synchronous = FULL</code></a>. A common tuned choice is WAL mode with <code>synchronous = NORMAL</code>, which skips the flush at commit. The database stays consistent, but a transaction that committed can roll back after a power loss or operating-system crash.</p>

      <h3 id="choosing-loss">Choosing what may be lost</h3>
      <p>The settings are cheap to change and easy to regret, so decide per kind of data what its last few seconds are worth. Ask what losing them costs, and whether the data can be recovered another way: from an upstream queue or an event log, or simply rebuilt.</p>
      <p>Asynchronous commit suits some data and not other data in the same database. That is why PostgreSQL lets you set the commit mode for one transaction. InnoDB’s setting is server-wide, and SQLite’s <code>synchronous</code> pragma applies to each connection.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each kind of data can tolerate losing"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Kind of data</th><th>What losing the last moments costs</th><th>A reasonable setting</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Payments, orders, anything the user has seen confirmed</td><td>A confirmation that can’t be honoured, and no record to rebuild it from</td><td>The default, with full durability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Page-view and click counters</td><td>A few seconds of counts nobody will miss</td><td>Asynchronous commit, for those transactions only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Events that can be replayed from a queue or an upstream log</td><td>Replay from the source after a crash</td><td>Asynchronous commit, provided you can tell where to resume</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Caches, scratch tables, test and CI databases, bulk loads that can restart</td><td>Rebuild from scratch</td><td>Unlogged tables, or flushing off for a throwaway database</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Asynchronous commit never tells the application anything. <code>COMMIT</code> returns success either way, and the loss only shows up after a crash, by comparing with what the application remembers. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling conflicts and uncertain outcomes</a> covers the first row’s problem from the application’s side.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="with-replicas"><h2 id="with-replicas">Synchronous replication</h2>
      <p>A commit is as durable as the places it has been flushed. A copy on another machine can stand in for the local flush, or add to it, but only if the commit waits for it.</p>
      <p>In PostgreSQL, when synchronous standbys are configured, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT`}><code>synchronous_commit</code></a> chooses how far the standby must get before <code>COMMIT</code> returns: <code>remote_write</code>, <code>on</code> or <code>remote_apply</code>. At each of these levels, the primary also waits for its own flush.</p>
      <p>The figure follows the commit record from the primary to a standby. Choose what <code>COMMIT</code> waits for, and watch what the standby holds when the primary’s disk fails just after the reply.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="sync-standby"
        number={14}
        title="PostgreSQL: how far the standby gets before COMMIT returns"
        alt="Five layers, top to bottom: the primary’s WAL buffers and WAL file, then the standby’s operating-system cache, WAL file and replayed copy of page 12."
        caption="The primary’s disk fails just after the reply in every case. An asynchronous standby is usually only moments behind; the drawing shows the failure arriving within those moments."
      />
      <p>With <em>primary flushed</em>, the standby is asynchronous: the reply came before the record left the primary, so the failover lost a commit the application had been told about. Each of the other three choices kept it. With <em>standby wrote</em>, though, the record was only in the standby’s operating-system cache when the reply came, so a crash of that operating system at the same moment would have lost it.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each synchronous_commit level waits for on the standby"><table>
        <thead><tr><th><code>synchronous_commit</code></th><th><code>COMMIT</code> waits until the standby has</th><th>The commit survives on the standby</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>remote_write</code></td><td>Written the commit record to its file system</td><td>A crash of the standby’s PostgreSQL, but not of its operating system</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>on</code></td><td>Flushed the commit record to durable storage</td><td>A crash or power cut on the standby</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>remote_apply</code></td><td>Flushed and applied it, so queries there see the change</td><td>A crash or power cut on the standby</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>MySQL’s <a href={`${mysql}replication-semisync.html`}>semisynchronous replication</a> is a plugin that has to be installed and enabled on the source and the replicas. It makes a commit wait until at least one replica has acknowledged receiving the transaction, which a replica does after writing it to its relay log and flushing.</p>
      <p>By default the wait comes after the source has synced its binary log and before it commits to InnoDB. If no replica answers within the timeout, the source falls back to asynchronous replication.</p>
      <p>An ordinary asynchronous replica, in either engine, behaves like <em>primary flushed</em> in the figure. After a failover to it, commits that had not yet been shipped are gone, although the application was told they succeeded. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">Replication and failover</a> covers what each level promises and how failover chooses a new primary.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="after-commit"><h2 id="after-commit">What the application knows after COMMIT</h2>
      <p>The commonest way to lose track of a commit has nothing to do with storage. The application sends <code>COMMIT</code> and the connection drops: a network fault, a timeout, a restarted proxy. The server may be perfectly healthy. It may have received the command, committed, and failed to deliver the reply, or never have received it at all. The application cannot tell which, and the database has no way to make its reply arrive.</p>
      <p>A server crash adds the case Figure 8 showed. Either way, retrying blindly could apply the change twice, which matters more for a payment than for a price. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling conflicts and uncertain outcomes</a> shows how to make a retry safe.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="backups"><h2 id="backups">Backups and point-in-time recovery</h2>
      <p>Durability protects committed changes from a crash. It doesn’t protect them from losing the disk, or from a mistake. If the drive holding the table files and the log dies, there is nothing left to replay. If someone commits <code>DELETE FROM products</code>, crash recovery will faithfully keep the delete.</p>
      <p>A replica on another server covers a lost disk, but it copies the delete too. Undoing a mistake needs a copy from before it, and a way to bring that copy forward to just before the mistake.</p>
      <p><strong>Point-in-time recovery</strong> is crash recovery started from an older copy of the files, fed with a saved log, and stopped early.</p>

      <h3 id="copying">Copying a database that is running</h3>
      <p>A backup is a copy of the data files, and a busy database keeps changing them while the copy is made. When the copy finishes, it looks like the disk of a server that crashed: some pages are old, some are new, and one may have been caught mid-write.</p>
      <p>You already know what repairs that. The backup starts by forcing a checkpoint and noting its log position, and keeps every log record from there on. Recovery replays those records over the mixed files until they agree.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL also <a href={`${docs}continuous-archiving.html`}>forces <code>full_page_writes</code> on</a> while the backup runs, so a page torn during the copy has a full-page image to repair it.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s physical backup tools take the same route with the redo log. <a href="https://docs.percona.com/percona-xtrabackup/8.4/how-xtrabackup-works.html">Percona XtraBackup</a>, for example, remembers the log position where it starts. It copies the data files while a second process copies the redo records made in the meantime, and applies them in a prepare step.</p>

      <h3 id="archive">Keeping the log after the backup</h3>
      <p>The checkpoint section showed that log behind a checkpoint is recycled. A backup taken at 09:00 is only a starting point: the changes made after it live in the log, and the log is about to be reused.</p>
      <p>Archiving copies each log file somewhere safe before that happens. PostgreSQL runs an <code>archive_command</code> on every completed WAL file, and recycles the file only once the command reports success. The archive then holds the whole history since the backup.</p>
      <p>Its health is worth watching. If archiving fails, the archive has a gap, and no restore can replay past it. <code>pg_stat_archiver</code> reports when the last file was archived and how many attempts have failed.</p>

      <h3 id="replay">Replaying, then stopping early</h3>
      <p>Take the shop’s day. A base backup begins at 09:00. At 10:00 P7 is set to 22.00. At 14:00 someone commits <code>DELETE FROM products</code>, and at 14:02 a valid price change for another product, P8, commits. The delete is noticed at 14:30.</p>
      <p>To recover, start a new server from the 09:00 backup and give it the archive and a <strong>recovery target</strong>: where to stop. PostgreSQL accepts a time, a transaction ID, a log position or a name saved with <code>pg_create_restore_point()</code>.</p>
      <p>Recovery replays the records in order, exactly as after a crash, and stops at the target. Here the target is 13:59, just before the delete’s commit record. Watch what the restored copy keeps.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="restore-timeline"
        number={15}
        title="PostgreSQL: restoring to a point before a delete"
        alt="Two columns: the original server’s history from 09:00 to 14:02, and a restored copy that replays the archive up to 13:59."
        caption="Times are invented for the example. The copy at 09:00 has pages of mixed ages, which replay repairs."
      />
      <p>The delete’s commit record was never replayed, and by the rule from crash recovery a transaction without one never committed. So on the restored copy the products are back, with P7 at 22.00.</p>

      <h3 id="cost">What stopping early costs</h3>
      <p>Replay stops at a point in the log, not at a single transaction. The 14:02 price change for P8 committed after the target, so it is lost with the delete. Recovery can’t skip one bad transaction and keep the good ones that followed it. The application has to find the commits made after the target, from its own records or the original server’s logs, and apply them again.</p>
      <p>The restored server also starts a new history from 13:59, and the archive still holds the old one, whose log from 14:00 onward describes a different future. PostgreSQL gives each history a <a href={`${docs}continuous-archiving.html#BACKUP-TIMELINES`}>timeline</a>, a number that is part of every log file’s name, so the new history doesn’t overwrite the old one. If 13:59 turns out to be the wrong target, you can recover again from the same archive to a different point.</p>
      <p>Two practical consequences follow. Restoring means replaying every record since the base backup, so restore time grows with the log, which is why base backups are repeated.</p>
      <p>And when the disk dies, the commits at risk are those whose log hadn’t reached the archive yet. A quiet server can hold a few commits in a half-empty log file for a long time, so PostgreSQL’s <code>archive_timeout</code> forces a switch to a new file at least that often.</p>

      <h3 id="engines">In MySQL and SQLite</h3>
      <p>MySQL has the same shape with one difference. Its point-in-time step replays the <a href={`${mysql}point-in-time-recovery-binlog.html`}>binary log</a> on top of a restored backup, not the redo log. The binary log is kept for 30 days by default, which is the longest a backup can be bridged unless you change <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>. You choose the stop point with <code>mysqlbinlog --stop-datetime</code> or <code>--stop-position</code>, and events at or after a stop position are left out.</p>
      <p>SQLite keeps no log archive of its own. Copying its file while a transaction is in progress can produce a corrupt copy. Use the <a href={`${sqlite}backup.html`}>online backup API</a> instead, which the shell exposes as <code>.backup</code>, or <a href={`${sqlite}lang_vacuum.html`}><code>VACUUM INTO</code></a>; both make consistent snapshots. A tool outside SQLite such as <a href="https://litestream.io/how-it-works/">Litestream</a>, which ships WAL frames to storage, adds point-in-time restore.</p>
      <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backups and recovery</a> covers how to decide what to recover, how to run a restore drill, and what an incomplete archive looks like.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="loss"><h2 id="loss">What can lose a committed change</h2>
      <p>Gathering the sections above, these are the ways a change the application was told about can be lost.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Ways a committed change can be lost"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>How much can be lost</th><th>Database consistent afterwards?</th><th>How you would notice</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Asynchronous commit</td><td>A window:<ul class="cell-list"><li>PostgreSQL: about 600 ms at the default writer delay</li><li>InnoDB at 2: about a second, after an operating-system crash</li><li>InnoDB at 0: about a second, even after a crash of the MySQL process alone</li><li>SQLite in WAL mode at <code>NORMAL</code>: the most recent commits</li></ul></td><td>Yes</td><td>Only by comparing with what the application remembers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A drive that acknowledges flushes it hasn’t made</td><td>No limit</td><td>Not guaranteed: in the worst case the data can be corrupted</td><td>Only by a pull-the-plug test before it happens</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flushing switched off (<code>fsync = off</code>, <code>synchronous = OFF</code>)</td><td>No limit</td><td>No: the database can be corrupt beyond repair, because the order between log and pages is no longer enforced</td><td>Errors at the next start, or none</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL unlogged tables</td><td>All their rows after any crash</td><td>Yes: the table is empty</td><td>The table is empty after a crash or an immediate shutdown, and on a promoted standby</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Failover to an asynchronous replica</td><td>The commits not yet shipped</td><td>Yes</td><td>Replication lag before the failover</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Losing the disk, restoring an old backup, or committing a bad statement</td><td>Everything since the backup, or the mistake</td><td>Yes</td><td>Only a restore helps, as the section on backups explains</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3 id="checks">What to check</h3>
      <p>Each decision this article supports comes with one thing to check.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Storage decisions and what to check for each"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>What to check</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Which commit setting to run, and what it risks</td><td>The flush time, with <code>pg_test_fsync</code> or <code>fio</code>, against the commits a second you need</td><td><a href="#settings">Asynchronous commit</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>How large to let the log grow before a checkpoint</td><td>Timed against requested checkpoints in <code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code>, and the worst-case replay against your target</td><td><a href="#choosing-interval">Sizing the log between checkpoints</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Whether a drive’s flush can be trusted</td><td>A pull-the-plug test, or documented power-loss protection</td><td><a href="#flush-check">Checking your own storage</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>How long a restart after a crash will take</td><td>The replay rate, measured by crashing a copy under load</td><td><a href="#recovery-time">Recovery time</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>What would bring back a lost disk</td><td>The archive’s last success and failed attempts, in <code>pg_stat_archiver</code></td><td><a href="#archive">Keeping the log after the backup</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/watching-storage/">Watching storage in a running database</a> reads the views and log lines behind each of these in full.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="numbers"><h2 id="numbers">Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <p>The commit rates, the replay time and the log lines come from PostgreSQL 18.6 on one Apple-silicon laptop with its internal SSD. They are observations about that machine, and show the pattern rather than typical numbers.</p>
      <p>For the commit rates, <code>pgbench</code> ran a script that inserts one row per transaction. The settings were the defaults, except that <code>wal_sync_method</code> was <code>fsync_writethrough</code>, which asks the drive to flush its own write cache. With the default method, a flush returned in a fraction of a millisecond, because macOS acknowledges a plain flush before the drive has finished, and the laptop managed about 25,000 commits a second.</p>
      <p>For the replay rate, an update-heavy <code>pgbench</code> test ran for 20 seconds after a checkpoint and wrote 566 MiB (594 MB) of log. The server was then stopped with <code>pg_ctl stop -m immediate</code>, which skips the shutdown checkpoint much as a crash does, and started again. The checkpoint distances and the warning about frequent checkpoints come from the same load run for 45 seconds. Both runs were separate from the crash in Watching storage, which used another copy.</p>
      <p>The figures are models of documented behaviour, built from the engines’ manuals and PostgreSQL’s source. No MySQL server was available, so nothing about InnoDB was measured.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Checked on 17 and 18 October 2026. The article follows engines that change fixed-size pages in place and keep a log. Log-structured stores, which write sorted files instead of updating pages, the internals of filesystems, and distributed commit across servers are outside it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href={`${docs}continuous-archiving.html`}>Continuous Archiving and Point-in-Time Recovery</a>, <a href={`${docs}storage-page-layout.html`}>Database Page Layout</a>, <a href={`${docs}mvcc-intro.html`}>Concurrency Control</a>, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html`}>resource settings</a> for shared buffers and the background writer, <a href={`${docs}wal-intro.html`}>Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)</a>, <a href={`${docs}wal-reliability.html`}>Reliability</a>, <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>WAL Configuration</a>, <a href={`${docs}wal-async-commit.html`}>Asynchronous Commit</a>, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html`}>WAL settings</a>, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-replication.html`}>replication settings</a>, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-error-handling.html`}>error handling</a> and <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}>pg_test_fsync</a>: the worked example in this article.</li>
        <li>PostgreSQL source: <code>CalculateCheckpointSegments</code> in <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/REL_18_STABLE/src/backend/access/transam/xlog.c"><code>xlog.c</code></a> sets the log-size checkpoint trigger used in Sizing the log between checkpoints, rounded down to whole segments, and <code>XLogCheckpointNeeded</code> counts it from where the previous checkpoint began. The manual describes <code>max_wal_size</code> as a soft limit but does not give the formula.</li>
        <li>MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual: <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool.html`}>Buffer Pool</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-index-types.html`}>Clustered and Secondary Indexes</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-physical-structure.html`}>Physical Structure of an InnoDB Index</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-row-format.html`}>Row Formats</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-multi-versioning.html`}>Multi-Versioning</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-redo-log.html`}>Redo Log</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-checkpoints.html`}>Checkpoints</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>Buffer Pool Flushing</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-undo-logs.html`}>Undo Logs</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-purge-configuration.html`}>Purge Configuration</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-doublewrite-buffer.html`}>Doublewrite Buffer</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>InnoDB Recovery</a> and <a href={`${mysql}optimizing-innodb-transaction-management.html`}>Optimizing InnoDB Transaction Management</a>: how InnoDB does each step. <a href={`${mysql}innodb-parameters.html`}>InnoDB startup options and system variables</a>, <a href={`${mysql}server-status-variables.html`}>status variables</a>, <a href={`${mysql}binary-log.html`}>The Binary Log</a>, <a href={`${mysql}replication-options-binary-log.html`}>Binary Logging Options and Variables</a> and <a href={`${mysql}replication-semisync.html`}>Semisynchronous Replication</a> define the settings and counters, and <a href={`${mysql}point-in-time-recovery-binlog.html`}>Point-in-Time Recovery Using Binary Log</a> the restore. <a href="https://bugs.mysql.com/bug.php?id=73725">MySQL bug 73725</a> records that, at <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit = 2</code>, a crash of <code>mysqld</code> alone loses nothing.</li>
        <li>SQLite: <a href={`${sqlite}atomiccommit.html`}>Atomic Commit</a> for the rollback journal and what it assumes about disks, <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>Write-Ahead Logging</a> and the <a href={`${sqlite}fileformat2.html`}>file format</a> for WAL mode and rowid tables, <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_synchronous`}>PRAGMA synchronous</a> and <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_wal_checkpoint`}>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}howtocorrupt.html`}>How To Corrupt An SQLite Database File</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}cksumvfs.html`}>the checksum extension</a>, and the <a href={`${sqlite}backup.html`}>online backup API</a>.</li>
